/* WHERE THE STATS TAB POINTS.
 *
 * TWO ADDRESSES FOR ONE REPORT, and they are not interchangeable.
 *
 *   · STATS_REPORT_URL is the report as a person visits it — the address out of the
 *     browser's own bar, with the workspace and the report section in the path. It is what
 *     "Open in Power BI" opens, in a real top-level tab, because that is the only context
 *     Microsoft sign-in can actually complete in.
 *   · STATS_EMBED_URL is /reportEmbed, the secure-embed address, which is the one Power BI
 *     supports being framed. The panel frames THAT, never the first: the visiting address
 *     is a full Power BI app shell — its own nav, its own chrome — and it is not meant to
 *     be rendered inside anything.
 *
 * autoAuth=true, AND THE SIGN-IN HOSTS ARE ALLOWED TO BE FRAMED. Both halves of that
 * sentence are the fix for a screen that rendered "This content is blocked. Contact the
 * site owner to fix the issue." over the whole report, and neither half works alone.
 *
 *   · THE FRAME WAS BLOCKED BEFORE IT EVER REACHED MICROSOFT. The panel document's own
 *     <meta> CSP said `frame-src 'none'` while the manifest said `app.powerbi.com`, and a
 *     browser enforces the INTERSECTION of the two — so the allowance was 'none' and the
 *     first load of the embed address was refused. That is the block in the screenshot.
 *   · AND IT WOULD HAVE BEEN BLOCKED AGAIN ONE REDIRECT LATER. /reportEmbed answers a
 *     browser that has no Power BI EMBED session — which is a different thing from being
 *     signed in to Microsoft, and is why "but I am logged in" and a blank report are not a
 *     contradiction — by sending the frame to login.microsoftonline.com. frame-src governs
 *     navigations INSIDE a frame, not only its first address, so the sign-in hosts have to
 *     be on the list or the flow stops halfway with nothing rendered.
 *
 * autoAuth=true is the parameter Power BI's own "Embed report → Website or portal" dialog
 * puts in the URL it generates. It is what makes that sign-in exchange happen silently for
 * a browser that already has a Microsoft session, instead of leaving a frame that shows
 * nothing until somebody presses Open in Power BI. Without it the embed page waits to be
 * driven by the powerbi-client SDK and an embed token, which is the OTHER embedding model
 * — it needs a service principal and a secret, and this panel deliberately holds neither.
 *
 * NO CREDENTIAL, anywhere in this. The panel holds no Microsoft token and asks for none;
 * the report is rendered for whoever the browser already is, the same posture the Copilot
 * bridge takes with the analyst's own session. The CSP change grants FRAMING and nothing
 * else — connect-src still names no Microsoft host, so this document cannot make a request
 * to one, and the frame stays unreadable and unmessageable from out here.
 *
 * STATS_TENANT_ID is optional and empty by default. Power BI's generated URL carries a
 * `ctid` naming the tenant the report lives in, which matters only to somebody signed in
 * to more than one (a guest account in another org): with it, the silent sign-in is aimed
 * at the right tenant instead of whichever one the browser used last. Blank means "the
 * tenant this browser is already in", which is right for everybody on the SOTI tenant.
 */
const STATS_GROUP_ID = 'f8779279-b9b6-4b54-984a-d7dde638eac5';
const STATS_REPORT_ID = '6c8f5ea0-4de2-4a28-9b3f-c1d091992764';
